      "id": "90b4e977-82cc-4cf4-9830-cf030ece8c46",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "This section is **very important** — here you move from *your own functional interface* to **Java’s built-in functional interfaces**.\nI’ll explain **what changed**, **why it matters**, and then go **step by step** through the code.\n\n---\n\n# 🧠 Big idea of Section 14.4\n\n> **You almost never need to create your own functional interface.**\n> Java already gives you most of them in `java.util.function`.\n\nThis file introduces:\n\n* `BinaryOperator<T>`\n* `BiConsumer<T, T>`\n* `Predicate<T>` (used indirectly via `removeIf`)\n\n---\n\n## 1️⃣ Everything at the top is already familiar\n\n```java\nList<String> list = new ArrayList<>(List.of(\n        \"alpha\", \"bravo\", \"charlie\", \"delta\"));\n```\n\nAnd these parts:\n\n```java\nfor (String s : list) { ... }\nlist.forEach(...)\n```\n\n👉 Same lambda concepts you already learned:\n\n* `forEach` uses **Consumer<T>**\n* Lambdas replace loops by *passing behavior*\n\nSo we’ll focus on **what’s new**.\n\n---\n\n## 2️⃣ Replacing your `Operation<T>` with `BinaryOperator<T>`\n\n### Old (Section 14.3)\n\n```java\nOperation<T> function\nfunction.operate(a, b)\n```\n\n### New (Section 14.4)\n\n```java\nBinaryOperator<T> function\nfunction.apply(a, b)\n```\n\n```java\npublic static <T> T calculator(BinaryOperator<T> function, T value1, T value2) {\n    T result = function.apply(value1, value2);\n    System.out.println(\"Result of operation: \" + result);\n    return result;\n}\n```\n\n### What is `BinaryOperator<T>`?\n\nIt is a **built-in functional interface**:\n\n```java\nT apply(T t1, T t2);\n```\n\nWhich is *exactly* what your `Operation<T>` was doing.\n\n✅ Same behavior\n✅ Less code\n✅ Standard Java\n\n---\n\n## 3️⃣ Using `BinaryOperator` with lambdas\n\n```java\nint result = calculator((var a, var b) -> a + b, 5, 2);\n```\n\n### Java infers:\n\n* `T = Integer`\n* Lambda becomes:\n\n```java\nInteger apply(Integer a, Integer b)\n```\n\n---\n\n```java\nvar result2 = calculator((a, b) -> a / b, 10.0, 2.5);\n```\n\n* `T = Double`\n\n---\n\n```java\nvar result3 = calculator(\n        (a, b) -> a.toUpperCase() + \" \" + b.toUpperCase(),\n        \"Ralph\", \"Kramden\");\n```\n\n* `T = String`\n\n🔑 **Same method, same interface, different meaning**\nThat’s the power of generics + lambdas.\n\n---\n\n## 4️⃣ Working with coordinates (new example)\n\n```java\nvar coords = Arrays.asList(\n        new double[]{47.2160, -95.2348},\n        new double[]{29.1566, -89.2495},\n        new double[]{35.1556, -90.0659});\n```\n\nEach entry is:\n\n```java\ndouble[] { latitude, longitude }\n```\n\nPrinting them:\n\n```java\ncoords.forEach(s -> System.out.println(Arrays.toString(s)));\n```\n\nThis uses:\n\n* `Consumer<double[]>`\n\n---\n\n## 5️⃣ Introducing `BiConsumer<T, T>`\n\n```java\nBiConsumer<Double, Double> p1 = (lat, lng) ->\n        System.out.printf(\"[lat:%.3f lon:%.3f]%n\", lat, lng);\n```\n\n### What is `BiConsumer`?\n\n```java\nvoid accept(T t1, T t2);\n```\n\n* Takes **two inputs**\n* Returns **nothing**\n\nPerfect for printing, logging, saving, etc.\n\n---\n\n## 6️⃣ Passing behavior as a parameter\n\n```java\nprocessPoint(firstPoint[0], firstPoint[1], p1);\n```\n\n```java\npublic static <T> void processPoint(T t1, T t2, BiConsumer<T,T> consumer) {\n    consumer.accept(t1, t2);\n}\n```\n\n### What this means\n\nYou are passing:\n\n* **data** → latitude & longitude\n* **behavior** → how to process them\n\nThis is the **Strategy Pattern**, simplified by lambdas.\n\n---\n\n## 7️⃣ Inline vs reusable lambdas\n\nReusable:\n\n```java\ncoords.forEach(s -> processPoint(s[0], s[1], p1));\n```\n\nInline:\n\n```java\ncoords.forEach(s -> processPoint(s[0], s[1],\n        (lat, lng) ->\n                System.out.printf(\"[lat:%.3f lon:%.3f]%n\", lat, lng)));\n```\n\nBoth are valid.\nChoose based on **readability and reuse**.\n\n---\n\n## 8️⃣ `removeIf` → Predicate<T>\n\n```java\nlist.removeIf(s -> s.equalsIgnoreCase(\"bravo\"));\n```\n\n### What is `removeIf` using?\n\n```java\nPredicate<T>\nboolean test(T t);\n```\n\nYour lambda:\n\n```java\ns -> s.equalsIgnoreCase(\"bravo\")\n```\n\nreturns `true` → element removed\nreturns `false` → element kept\n\n---\n\n### Another predicate example\n\n```java\nlist.removeIf(s -> s.startsWith(\"ea\"));\n```\n\nRemoves:\n\n```\neasy\nearnest\n```\n\n---\n\n## 9️⃣ The BIG lesson of this section\n\n### Java’s most important functional interfaces\n\n| Interface           | Method        | Purpose                      |\n| ------------------- | ------------- | ---------------------------- |\n| `Consumer<T>`       | `accept(T)`   | Do something                 |\n| `BiConsumer<T,U>`   | `accept(T,U)` | Do something with two values |\n| `Predicate<T>`      | `test(T)`     | true / false                 |\n| `Function<T,R>`     | `apply(T)`    | transform                    |\n| `BinaryOperator<T>` | `apply(T,T)`  | combine                      |\n\n---\n\n## 🔑 Mental model to keep forever\n\n> **Lambda = behavior**\n> **Functional interface = shape of that behavior**\n> **Generics = reuse across types**\n\n---\n\n## 🚀 Why this matters in real projects\n\nYou now understand how:\n\n* `removeIf`\n* `forEach`\n* Streams (`filter`, `map`, `reduce`)\n* Event handlers\n* Callbacks\n\n**all work internally**\n\n---\n\n### You are now fully ready for:\n\n* `Predicate` chaining\n* Method references (`System.out::println`)\n* Streams API\n\nIf you want, next I can:\n\n* rewrite this using **method references**\n* draw a **mental map of all functional interfaces**\n* show **real Spring Boot usage**\n\nJust tell me 👍\n",
      "metadata": {
